## When to write an ADR

Write one in `docs/architecture/decisions/` when a decision is hard to reverse,
affects multiple modules, or introduces a new technology. Use the next number
and the structure of `000-template.md`: context, options considered with why
each was rejected, decision, consequences (positive and cost), and when to
revisit.

### Reading an ADR's status

`Accepted` means the decision was agreed, **not** that the code exists. Only
`Implemented` means that.

- An ADR is `Implemented` only when every path in its **Implemented in** field
  exists and every command in its **Verified by** field exists in the repository
  and is run by CI.
- With open items under **Follow-ups**, treat the ADR as partially built.
- Before citing an ADR as a constraint, check its status and open the
  `Implemented in` paths. If it is `Proposed` or `Accepted`, the rule is not
  yet a fact about this repository. Say so instead of refusing to write the
  first implementation of it.
- **Must not** change an ADR's status without opening the paths and running the
  check. Never set `Implemented` from intent.
- **Must not** edit or delete a decided ADR. To reverse one, write a new ADR
  that `Supersedes` it and set the old one's status to
  `Superseded by ADR-NNN`.

The `adr-drift` CI gate runs `docs/architecture/decisions/verify.mjs`, which fails
when a path in `Implemented in` is missing, when a `Verified by` command does not
exist in this repository, or when a status is outside the allowed set.

